      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll one number that is less than 3 on the first die and one number that is greater than 3 on the second die, we need to follow four steps:\n\n1. First, identify the probability of rolling a number less than 3 on a single six-sided die. The numbers less than 3 are 1 and 2. Since there are 6 possible outcomes (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), the probability of rolling a number less than 3 (i.e. 1 or 2) on the first die is $Pr(\\text{die 1} < 3) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\n2. Next, identify the probability of rolling a number greater than 3 on a single six-sided die. The numbers greater than 3 are 4, 5, and 6. Thus, the probability of rolling a number greater than 3 on the second die is $Pr(\\text{die 2} > 3) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n3. Now, multiply the two probabilities from points 1 and 2 to find the combined probability of rolling one number less than 3 and one number greater than 3: $\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}$.\n\nAlternatively, you can solve the problem through brute force iteration. Look at all four cases:\n\nCase 1: Neither die rolls a number less than 3.\n\n$(1,1)$ \n\n$(1,2)$ \n\n$(2,1)$ \n\n$(2,2)$ \n\nThis case has 4 similarly improbable outcomes, drawing from 8 overall. The probability of these 4 outcomes is $\\frac{4}{36}$.\n\nCase 2: Die 1 does not roll a number less than 3 but die 2 does.\n\n$(1,4)$ \n\n$(1,5)$ \n\n$(1,6)$ \n\n$(2,4)$ \n\n$(2,5)$ \n\n$(2,6)$ \n\nThis case has 6 similarly unlikely outcomes, drawing from 18 overall. The probability of all these 6 outcomes is $\\frac{6}{36}$.\n\nCase 3: Die 1 rolls a number less than 3 and die 2 does not roll a number greater than 3.\n\nOnly one of the 12 outcomes for this case is less inclusive to Case 1, so this probability is $\\frac{1}{36}$. (Though overlooked by the previous heuristic.) \n\nCase 4: Both dice roll a number less than 3.\n\nTo be included in this probability, (1,2) and (2,1) below must be excluded/addressed in Case 1. A probability of $\\frac{2}{3}$ addresses this more inclusive range. \n\nAdding up the four cases: $\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{11}{36}}.$",
