      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we first need to understand the individual probabilities for each event and then find the combined probability of these two independent events happening simultaneously.\n\n1. **Probability of Rolling a Number Less Than Three on the First Die:**\n   - A six-sided die has numbers 1 through 6.\n   - The numbers less than three are 1 and 2.\n   - Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes (1 and 2) out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   - The probability is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{1 or 2 on the first die}) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Probability of Rolling a Number Greater Than Three on the Second Die:**\n   - The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6.\n   - Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes (4, 5, and 6).\n   - The probability is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{4 or 5 or 6 on the second die}) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Combined Probability of Both Events Happening:**\n   - Since the rolls of the two dice are independent events, we multiply their probabilities.\n   - The combined probability \\( P \\) is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{\"1 or 2 on the first die and 4 or 5 or 6 on the second die\"}) = P(\\text{1 or 2 on the first die}) \\times P(\\text{4 or 5 or 6 on the second die})\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     P = \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
