      "text": "There are 6 numbers on each side of the six-sided die, so there are a total of 6 * 6 = 36 possible outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice.\nTo roll a number less than three, Nathan must roll a 1 or 2 on the first die. There are two favorable outcomes for the first die. \nTo roll a number greater than three, Nathan must roll a 4, 5, or 6 on the second die. There are 3 favorable outcomes for the second die.\nSo, the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is (2/6) * (3/6) = 1/3.\nThe final answer is \\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}.",
